/*!
@brief The contract every socket honors, whatever transport it speaks.

A socket is a `Closable` that also knows where it sits in the network: the
address it occupies locally, and the descriptor the system knows it by. That is
all any two sockets have in common, so it is all this asks for.

What a socket carries is said elsewhere, by whoever can honor it. A connection
is a stream of bytes, so it adds `Readable` and `Writeable` from `io/traits`;
something that only waits for connections carries nothing, and something that
exchanges whole messages is not a stream and must not be made to look like one.
Each picks up the traits it can keep, and this one underneath them all.
*/

import Closable from "io/traits"

/**
@brief A resource bound to a network address, with a system descriptor behind it.

Extends `Closable` because every socket holds a resource the system wants back.
Closing is idempotent, so a socket can be handed to `defer` the moment it
exists, before it is bound or connected to anything.

Deliberately small. `fileno` and `local_addr` are the two questions that have
an answer for any socket at all; a peer address, a backlog, a message boundary
do not, and belong to the class that has one rather than up here.

@see Closable
*/
pub trait Socket impl Closable {
    /**
    @brief Return the descriptor the system knows this socket by.

    For what the socket API does not wrap: an option no method covers, or a
    question only the system can answer.

    Do not read from it, write to it, or close it. The event loop owns the
    readiness of that descriptor and the operations queued against it, and
    going behind its back takes bytes that belong to a queued read, or hands
    the number back to the system while the loop is still watching it. Use
    `close` for closing.

    @return The descriptor, as the number the system knows it by.

    @panic OSError When the socket is closed, or has no descriptor yet.

    @see close
    */
    func fileno()

    /**
    @brief Return the local address the socket occupies.

    @return The local address, as a Sockaddr.

    @panic OSError  When the socket is closed.
    */
    func local_addr()
}
